is of LLMs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08813)\nciteturn2814view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08813\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 160\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 9 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:The Model's Language Matters: A Comparative Privacy Analysis of LLMs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Abhishek K. Mishra , cite6†Antoine Boutet , cite7†Lucas Magnana L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled The Model's Language Matters: A Comparative Privacy Analysis of LLMs, by Abhishek K. Mishra and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed across multilingual applications that handle sensitive data, yet their scale and linguistic variability introduce major privacy risks. Mostly evaluated for English, this paper investigates how language structure affects privacy leakage in LLMs trained on English, Spanish, French, and Italian medical corpora.\nL17: We quantify six linguistic indicators and evaluate three attack vectors: extraction, counterfactual memorization, and membership inference. Results show that privacy vulnerability scales with linguistic redundancy and tokenization granularity: Italian exhibits the strongest leakage, while English shows higher membership separability. In contrast, French and Spanish display greater resilience due to higher morphological complexity.\nL18: Overall, our findings provide the first quantitative evidence that language matters in privacy leakage, underscoring the need for language-aware privacy-preserving mechanisms in LLM deployments.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2510.08813 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2510.08813v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.08813†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Abhishek Kumar Mishra [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 9 Oct 2025 20:59:42 UTC (359 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled The Model's Language Matters: A Comparative Privacy Analysis of LLMs, by Abhishek K. Mishra and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite8†View PDF L35:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-10 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: cite22†cs.CR L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite25†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL74: \nL75: Bibliographic Explorer (cite26†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL76: \nL77: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL78: \nL79: Connected Papers (cite27†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL80: \nL81: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL82: \nL83: Litmaps (cite28†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL84: \nL85: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL86: \nL87: scite Smart Citati