org/abs/2510.09259)\nciteturn2814view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09259\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Oct 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 18 Mar 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Detecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training for Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Yongding Tao , cite7†Tian Wang , cite8†Yihong Dong , cite9†Huanyu Liu , cite10†Kechi Zhang , cite11†Xiaolong Hu , cite12†Ge Li L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training for Large Language Models, by Yongding Tao and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Data contamination poses a significant threat to the reliable evaluation of Large Language Models (LLMs). This issue arises when benchmark samples may inadvertently appear in training sets, compromising the validity of reported performance. While detection methods have been developed for the pre-training and Supervised Fine-Tuning stages, a critical research gap exists for the increasingly significant phase of Reinforcement Learning (RL) post-training.\nL17: As RL post-training becomes pivotal for advancing LLM reasoning, the absence of specialized contamination detection methods in this paradigm presents a critical vulnerability. To address this, we conduct the first systematic study of data detection within RL post-training scenario and propose Self-Critique. Our method is motivated by a key observation: after RL phase, the output entropy distribution of LLMs tends to collapse into highly specific and sparse modes.\nL18: Self-Critique probes for the underlying policy collapse, i.e., the model's convergence to a narrow reasoning path, which causes this entropy reduction. To facilitate this research, we also introduce RL-MIA, a benchmark constructed to simulate this specific contamination scenario. Extensive experiments show that Self-Critique significantly outperforms baseline methods across multiple models and contamination tasks, achieving an AUC improvement of up to 30%.\nL19: Whereas existing methods are close to a random guess for RL-phase contamination, our method makes detection possible.\nL20: Comments:  | Accepted to ICLR 2026. Code is available at cite15†this https URL†github.com L21: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2510.09259 [cs.CL]\nL23:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2510.09259v2 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL24:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.09259†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Yongding Tao [cite19†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Fri, 10 Oct 2025 10:58:50 UTC (496 KB)\nL29: [v2] Wed, 18 Mar 2026 06:50:38 UTC (373 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Data Contamination from Reinforcement Learning Post-training for Large Language Models, by Yongding Tao and 6 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite13†View PDF L38:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite20†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite21†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.CL\nL45: \nL46: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L47: \nL48: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025-10 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite27†cs L53: cite28†cs.AI L54: cite29†cs.LG L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite30†NASA AD